WHAT THE GUIDE OFFERS
---------------------
MiniMax prompts officially support multiple shots inside one generated clip:

  [Shot 1] ... (no timestamp on the first shot)
  [Shot 2] At 00:05.000, the camera cuts to...

Rules from the guide:
- Sequential shot numbers; every shot after the first starts with a strictly
  increasing cut time formatted HH:MM.mmm (e.g. "At 00:03.500"), within the
  clip duration.
- Cut verbs: "the camera cuts to", "the shot cuts to", "the shot transitions
  to", "the shot changes to", "the shot switches to"; cross-dissolve / fade /
  wipe only when explicitly requested.
- A cut must introduce NEW information (subject, space, state, viewpoint, or
  time). If only distance or a slight angle changes, prefer camera motion
  instead of a cut.
- Dialogue crossing a cut: <scenetrans> at both connecting points + an explicit
  "continues seamlessly across the cut" style statement. <cutoff> when speech
  is truncated by the end of the video.
- FL2VA (first+last frame references) generally favors a SINGLE shot so the
  model can interpolate continuously between the frames.

WHY IT IS INTERESTING FOR SHORT-FORM CONTENT
--------------------------------------------
- Instagram/YouTube pacing thrives on cuts: punch-ins on dialogue lines,
  reaction-shot alternation, hard cuts on story-object reveals. Our current
  output is one continuous 10-second push-in per part, which reads slow next
  to native IG editing.
- A 2-3 shot part (e.g. wide action beat -> close-up reaction) could deliver
  the escalation/turn grammar our treatment plans without waiting for part
  boundaries.

WHY IT CONFLICTS WITH THE CURRENT PIPELINE (the reason it is deferred)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
1. FRAME LOCKING: animator.py chains parts by exporting part-N-end.jpg and
   feeding it as the mandatory opening frame of part N+1 (I2VA), and on
   regeneration also locks the closing frame (FL2VA). Multishot makes the
   "final frame" of a part a cut-dependent composition that is much harder to
   predict and match — continuity between parts degrades when the last shot's
   framing is not the thing the script planned to hand off.
2. TREATMENT ASSUMPTIONS: the Director's Treatment escalation ladder is built
   on one-shot-per-segment (one camera move per segment, held beats as Static
   Shots). Multishot requires re-planning how the ladder maps to cuts.
3. LITERAL MODEL RISK: each added cut is another place for the model to
   duplicate characters, drop backgrounds, or break style consistency. Single
   shot is the safe baseline until proven otherwise.
4. BUDGET: cut choreography + <scenetrans> continuity statements cost
   characters in a 6,000-char file.

PRECONDITIONS BEFORE REVISITING
---------------------------------
- Single-shot outputs with the three-core-field format (integrated_multimodal_
  description / overall_soundscape / non_diegetic_music) are validated as
  reliably filmable in manual testing.
- A test scene is generated with one deliberately multishot part and its
  cross-part continuity frames are inspected: does part N's final shot land
  where part N+1's first-frame anchor expects?

IMPLEMENTATION SKETCH (when revisiting)
---------------------------------------
- scriptgen/prompts.py: lift the "exactly one shot per segment" rule behind a
  flag (e.g. --shots N or a per-scene movie.yml field); teach the [Shot 2]
  At 00:05.000 cut grammar, cut-verb vocabulary, and the "new information"
  requirement; require the treatment to place cuts on the ladder rungs.
- scriptgen/prompts.py dialogue rules: add <scenetrans> across-cut continuity
  statements and <cutoff> handling.
- scriptgen/format.py: no change expected (the body is already the raw
  multimodal description).
- animator/: verify end-frame continuity logic still holds when the final shot
  is a cut target; consider forcing the LAST shot of every multishot part to
  be the hand-off composition, stated explicitly ("[Shot N] ends on ...").
- Tests: new parsing tests for multi-[Shot] bodies; animator continuity tests.
